WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.510
RETO MEIER: Today I learned
that when observed globally,

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:04.610
weather station records
show an increase

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:06.950
in average daily temperatures
of over half a degree

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:10.300
Celsius each decade
since the 1970s.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.230
I'm Reto Meier, Google
developer advocate.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:14.390
Join me as we explore
the NOAA weather data

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:15.310
sets with BigQuery.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:23.190
Climate science can be
a controversial topic.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:24.860
So when we told
Savio Lawrence we

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:26.870
were putting his
analysis on YouTube,

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:29.375
he asked us to
preserve his anonymity.

00:00:29.375 --> 00:00:32.009
SAVIO LAWRENCE: You
just told them my name.

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:33.407
Am I in this shot?

00:00:33.407 --> 00:00:34.490
RETO MEIER: It'll be fine.

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:36.410
We'll fix it in post.

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:40.256
So tell us, how did you go
about performing your analysis?

00:00:40.256 --> 00:00:42.380
SAVIO LAWRENCE: So I found
the weather data already

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:44.960
present in BigQuery's
public data set project.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.370
Then I used two great products--

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:48.320
BigQuery and Tableau-- together.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.300
BigQuery includes two
weather station data

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.640
sets, the Global
Summary of Data, GSOD,

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:56.210
and Global Historical
Climatological Network, GHCN.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:59.140
Basically, anyone
can add data to GSOD,

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:02.760
while GHCN observations are
subject to a common suit of QA

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:03.740
reviews.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.130
I prepped and connected
this BigQuery data sources

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:09.000
to Tableau for
visualization and analysis.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.550
The first thing I
noticed was a trend

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:12.410
of increasing
average temperatures

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:16.510
each decade for both data sets,
which led to further analysis.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.370
RETO MEIER: Climate
science is complicated.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:22.940
So let's dive into the
details of this analysis.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.040
Many variables can
influence our results.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.650
Our data sets are particularly
susceptible to bias

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:29.960
from the number of
stations reporting,

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:31.850
their relative locations,
and the frequency

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:33.350
of their observations.

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:36.800
For our analysis to be
meaningful, we start in 1973,

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.300
the year after
which there aren't

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.150
big jumps in the number
of reporting stations,

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.950
and there's a reasonable
global coverage of stations.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:45.920
Our averages include
every station

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.410
with at least 300
measurements in a given year

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:50.590
to avoid seasonal bias.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:52.850
This still only tells
us part of the story.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:54.830
Global temperature
isn't consistent.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:58.220
Some places may have gotten
warmer, and others cooler.

00:01:58.220 --> 00:01:59.810
The distribution of
reporting stations

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.480
shows us that there are more
stations in the most heavily

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.160
populated latitudes.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:06.410
As a result, the regions
with the most stations

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:08.240
will have a
disproportionate influence

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.300
on the overall climate trends.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.890
To begin to account for this,
we can break our analysis

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:16.790
into smaller slices of the
data, based on latitude bands.

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:18.500
Stations here in
the Arctic Circle

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.590
show a significant increase
in average temperature

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:22.620
each decade--

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:26.960
1.23 degrees Celsius per decade
for GSOD, and 0.67 degrees

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.090
Celsius for GHCN.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:31.850
The northern temperate zone
includes the largest number

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:35.660
of stations, and represents
the most highly populated zone.

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.690
As with the Arctic Circle,
both the GSOD and GHCN data

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:41.800
sets show a gradual increase
in the average temperature,

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:46.530
with 0.68 and 0.65
increases respectively.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.890
When we reach the tropics
things get interesting.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:51.020
These graphs show that
the average temperature

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:53.870
has remained fairly constant
across both data sets,

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:57.140
with an average increase
of 0.31 and 0.28

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:58.820
per decade in the
northern tropics,

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:02.930
and just 0.19 and 0.28
in the southern tropics.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:04.970
Once we hit the
southern temperate zone,

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:09.110
both data sets saw a small but
gradual decrease each decade

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:10.430
until now.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:12.980
Record temperatures
in 2015 and 2016

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:16.699
have largely erased the
drop since the 1970s.

00:03:16.699 --> 00:03:18.740
Are the last two years
outliers, or the beginning

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.890
of a new warming trend for
the southern temperate zone?

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:25.010
It's too early to say based
on only two data points.

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:28.040
But it's an observation
worth tracking.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.920
Finally, we reach the Antarctic,
where the number of stations

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:34.250
is extremely low compared to
the other latitude bands, which

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:36.590
creates a noisier data set.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:39.380
The measurements we do have
indicate a significant cooling

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:41.090
trend each decade.

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:44.060
One possible influence could
be the increase in reporting

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:46.460
stations closer to
the South Pole that

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:49.490
have a lower temperature than
those located further out.

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:51.950
We can try to unwrap this
by further segmenting

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:52.920
the Antarctic Circle.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.340
But for now, we'll add it
to the list of observations

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:58.080
that need more follow-up.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.459
When measuring something as
complex as climate change,

00:04:00.459 --> 00:04:02.000
it's important to
remember that there

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.330
are many variables that can
influence and bias our results.

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:07.490
So while making
observations is easy,

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:09.350
drawing conclusions isn't.

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:12.410
Analyzing raw data like this can
provide useful starting points

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:15.200
to form hypotheses, and
begin further investigations

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.019
into the broader trends
and potential causes

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:19.940
that they may represent.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:21.950
As always, your first
terabyte of processing

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:23.220
each month is free.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.010
So I invite you to
open up BigQuery,

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:28.005
and do your own analyses, and
share your results with us.

00:04:28.005 --> 00:04:29.630
And as always,
subscribe to our channel

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:31.213
and follow our blog
to learn something

00:04:31.213 --> 00:04:32.816
new every week with BigQuery.

00:04:32.816 --> 00:04:34.190
SAVIO LAWRENCE:
You know, I think

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:36.740
it's OK if you just
tell people who I am.

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:38.579
I don't think this is
that controversial.

00:04:38.579 --> 00:04:39.620
RETO MEIER: You're right.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:41.120
Plus, we're safe
here in the bunker.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:45.390
SAVIO LAWRENCE: Seriously, we
are in a bunker [INAUDIBLE].

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:46.450
Thanks for watching.

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:47.450
Click here to subscribe.

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:50.830
And be sure to check out
some other videos here.

